Author a **confetti pop**, a 128x128 screen-space particle effect, using only the
particle tool.

It is a **celebratory confetti burst** — a party popper going off: a pop at the
center throws many small, colorful confetti pieces up and outward; they tumble and
spin, then flutter down under gravity and air drag, swaying side to side as they
fall and fading near the end, all in about 1.5 seconds. You are authoring a
**planar, screen-space** effect (width and height only, no depth).

## How the tool works

You do **not** place individual particles. You **author a system** — emitters,
forces, and per-particle curves — that the review UI and a game **simulate live**,
the way a real particle editor (Niagara, VFX Graph) plays a system. The only way
to shape the effect is the `particle-2d` binary already on your `PATH`. It is the
sole channel: you build the system by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered list of operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — is the
**authoritative output that is scored**, together with the system it emits.
Anything produced any other way is discarded.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags. Run `particle-2d --help` to
list every operation and `particle-2d <operation> --help` for one operation's exact
flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. The field dimensions, the
duration, and the fps are already seeded alongside the workspace at
`{{workspace}}`, so no operation needs those flags.

Rendering is a separate, on-request step: run **`particle-2d render`** to simulate
the system over its duration, write the preview GIF, and **emit the `system.json`**
the result is built from. Render to check your progress — and, crucially, **before
you finish**, so the system is not left empty. Because the effect is simulated
live, it varies slightly from play to play; author it to read the same across
replays.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Author the emitter system operation by operation until `particle-2d render` plays
the effect the brief describes — a centered pop that throws colorful confetti up
and outward, which tumbles and flutters down under gravity and air drag, swaying as
it falls and fading to a nearly empty field by the end. Run `render` to emit the
system, then stop: the recorded `actions.json` and the emitted `system.json` are
your finished submission.
